// =============================================================================
// tl_saturate.gendsp  --  gen~ DSL for the SATURATE module of `tape-loss`
// =============================================================================
//
// Module:        tl_saturate (Generation Loss MKII "SATURATE" knob +
//                hidden INPUT GAIN selector).
// Authored:      2026-04-27 by pedal-engineer (translation, not redesign).
// Source files (sha256, captured at authoring time):
//   docs/design-docs/dsp/tl-saturate-design.md
//     2d69115367e28c376194d24d0ae9087b58d56a0bd8b66b9e1832d379e93fb40e
//   dsp/reference/tl_saturate.py
//     1c798f732c4d53ed36a17ee9b062e0162ad2d808cd61b4b2f9c3c8bc09ce2b4a
//   data/tl_saturate_coefficients.json
//     4ab5994a40f510a00b00bd95c17a4bb0cd7c80efc7127840af2ff0feaf674b1b
//
// I/O CONTRACT (matches device/tape-loss/tl_saturate.maxpat stub):
//   in1 : signal L (audio)
//   in2 : signal R (audio)
//   In3 : pin0 -- routed to Param `saturate`   (0..1)
//   In4 : pin1 -- routed to Param `input_gain` (0=LINE, 1=INSTRUMENT, 2=HIGH_GAIN)
//   out1: signal L
//   out2: signal R
//
// (The maxpat stub wires inlet-3/inlet-4 to gen~ inlets 3/4. Inside this
//  codebox we expose `saturate` and `input_gain` as Params so an outer
//  [js] / [pattr] system can drive them. The two extra signal inlets are
//  declared but unused so the patch stays conformant; remove them in a
//  later pass once the integration agent decides on the param-routing
//  topology.)
//
// PARAMS:
//   saturate    : 0..1, default 0.0   -- knob position (quadratic taper inside)
//   input_gain  : 0|1|2, default 0    -- LINE / INSTRUMENT / HIGH_GAIN
//
// COEFFICIENT HOT-SWAP CONTRACT (with the [js] companion):
//   This file hardcodes the 44.1 kHz coefficient set from
//   data/tl_saturate_coefficients.json (`by_sample_rate.44100`).
//   The engineer's [js] companion -- which subscribes to `dspstate~` --
//   will, on a sample-rate change, send `setcoef <b0> <b1> <b2> <a1> <a2>`
//   style messages and `setdcr <R>` to a wrapper that re-binds the Params
//   below.  Until that companion lands the gen~ runs with 44.1k math even
//   at 48k; the audible drift is < 0.1 dB at 3 kHz (verified offline) so
//   it's tolerable for v0 sandbox bring-up.
//
//   Coefficient Params (declared as Param so the [js] companion can hot-swap):
//     pre_b0, pre_b1, pre_b2, pre_a1, pre_a2
//     de_b0, de_b1, de_b2, de_a1, de_a2
//     dcr_R
//   Halfband taps stay hardcoded; they are sample-rate-independent.
//
// SANITY CHECK POINTS (expected outputs from the numpy reference):
//   1. saturate=0, any input_gain, any input:
//        Out == In  (bit-identical bypass; design doc tradeoff #16,
//        verified in dsp/reference/tl_saturate.py::_run_property_checks
//        as cascade_err_db_at_1khz_sat0 = 0.00 dB).
//   2. saturate=0.7, input_gain=LINE, 1 kHz sine @ 0.5 amp:
//        THD ~= 0.211 (21.1 % — pronounced asymmetric drive).
//        Even-harmonic content (H2 amp ~= 62 in arbitrary FFT units)
//        emerges from the (1+beta*u) negative-half multiplier.
//   3. saturate=1.0, input_gain=LINE, 1 kHz sine @ 0.5 amp:
//        THD ~= 0.289 (28.9 % — crushed).
//        DC offset of unblocked signal ~= 7.5e-4 -- the DC blocker keeps
//        steady-state output DC near zero.
//
// ALGORITHM (chain matches dsp/reference/tl_saturate.py::_process_one_channel):
//
//     INPUT
//       v
//     [INPUT_GAIN preamp]                LINE / +6dB / +12dB
//       v
//   if (saturate <= 0) -> bypass to OUTPUT (with INPUT_GAIN compensation)
//       v
//     [Pre-emphasis high-shelf]          +4 dB / 3 kHz / Q=0.7  (RBJ, DFI)
//       v
//     [2x upsample (31-tap halfband FIR, polyphase)]
//       v
//     [Asymmetric tanh waveshaper]       (1/tanh(d))*tanh(d*u) * (u<0 ? 1+beta*u : 1)
//       v
//     [2x downsample (31-tap halfband FIR, polyphase)]
//       v
//     [De-emphasis high-shelf]           -4 dB / 3 kHz / Q=0.7  (RBJ, DFI)
//       v
//     [DC blocker]                       y = x - x1 + R*y1
//       v
//     [Drive-dependent output makeup]    -0.70 * 20*log10(d/tanh(d))
//       v
//     [INPUT_GAIN output cancellation]   * 10^(-INPUT_GAIN_dB/20)
//       v
//     OUTPUT
//
// IMPLEMENTATION NOTES / INTERPRETATION CALLS:
//
// 1. Mis-bias inject (drive>8) is OMITTED in v0 gen DSL.
//    The numpy reference seeds an RNG and adds a 0.5 Hz random walk,
//    capped at +/-0.002 (-54 dBFS). gen~ has `noise` (white) and
//    `noise()` is per-sample so we *could* low-pass it with `t60`,
//    but the spec says "tiny random walk added at drive>8" and at
//    -54 dBFS, below the DC blocker cutoff of 20 Hz it gets mostly
//    nulled. The audible difference is < -60 dBFS even at saturate=1.
//    Decision: defer to a later integration pass once the [js]
//    companion is wired and we can route a deterministic seed.
//    This is the only knowing divergence from the numpy ref.
//
// 2. Polyphase halfband (interp + decim).
//    The 31-tap halfband FIR has h[k] == 0 for all even k except k=15
//    (center). So:
//      - Upsampler output even phase  = h[15] * x[n - 7]   (delay = 15//2)
//      - Upsampler output odd phase   = sum over odd k of h[k] * x[n - ?]
//    We implement both phases as taps off a shared input delay line of
//    length 16 (we need x[n], x[n-1], ..., x[n-15] for the odd-phase
//    convolution). Decimator is the dual: feed both shaped sub-samples
//    into a 2x-rate delay line of length 31, output = sum h[k]*v[k]
//    every native sample.
//
//    The numpy ref multiplies the upsampler output by 2.0 to compensate
//    for zero-insertion energy loss (so that an all-pass cascade of
//    upsample->shaper(identity)->downsample preserves RMS).  We do the
//    same here: the *odd* phase already integrates that factor by
//    summing 14 non-trivial taps; we apply the *2.0 to both phases
//    explicitly to match the ref exactly.
//
// 3. Stereo: L and R use shared parameters and shared coefficient state
//    but independent signal-path state (separate History / Delay
//    instances). This matches the numpy ref's _process_one_channel()
//    invocation per channel.
//
// 4. Denormal flush: gen~ on M1 ARM doesn't have x86 denormal slowdowns,
//    but we still mirror the ref's `if (|y| < 1e-30) y = 0` for biquad
//    states to make A/B with the numpy reference exact.
//
// 5. saturate=0 short-circuit. gen~ has no compile-time static-if; we
//    use a runtime mix:  out = bypass*y_dry + (1-bypass)*y_wet, where
//    bypass = (saturate <= 0). This costs two extra muls/sample. Keeps
//    the spec's bit-identical bypass guarantee at the bottom of the knob.
//    NOTE: when bypass=1 we still feed zeros into the wet path to keep
//    the biquad / FIR state from accumulating denormal noise during the
//    bypass period; they recover within ~31 samples when the user turns
//    the knob back up. (Tradeoff: a tiny 31-sample fade-in transient on
//    knob-touch from 0 -> nonzero. The design doc accepts this in §10.)
//
// 6. input_gain is read as a continuous Param and quantized to 0/1/2 by
//    the [js] companion *before* it reaches gen. Inside gen we just
//    branch on the integer value via two `==` comparisons. dB->linear
//    conversion is hardcoded:
//      LINE       : 1.000000 (= 10^(  0/20))
//      INSTRUMENT : 1.995262 (= 10^(  6/20))
//      HIGH_GAIN  : 3.981072 (= 10^( 12/20))
//    Output cancellation:  1/g_in.
//
// =============================================================================


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Parameter declarations
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

// saturate (in3) and input_gain (in4) come via signal-rate inlets from
// the parent maxpat (dial-saturate → subpatcher inlet 2 → gen~ inlet 2,
// tab-input_gain → subpatcher inlet 3 → gen~ inlet 3). These are
// per-sample control values; they vary continuously when the user
// touches the knob, but are otherwise constant.
saturate = in3;
input_gain = in4;

// Pre-emphasis biquad coefficients (44.1 kHz; hot-swappable via [js])
Param pre_b0(1.4796626808353215);
Param pre_b1(-2.168322256291102);
Param pre_b2(0.857216222713986);
Param pre_a1(-1.3370165564518925);
Param pre_a2(0.5055732037100982);

// De-emphasis biquad coefficients (44.1 kHz; hot-swappable via [js])
Param de_b0(0.6758297096710346);
Param de_b1(-0.9035955111722491);
Param de_b2(0.3416813914808506);
Param de_a1(-1.4654166009424578);
Param de_a2(0.5793321909220942);

// DC blocker pole radius (44.1 kHz; hot-swappable via [js])
Param dcr_R(0.9971545388743885);

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Halfband FIR taps (31 taps, Kaiser beta=8.0, sample-rate-independent)
// Source: data/tl_saturate_coefficients.json -> halfband_fir.taps
// Even taps (except k=15) are zero -- the polyphase ops below skip them.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Center tap (k=15)
hb_15 = 0.49998341447554523;

// Odd-side taps (mirrored: hb_k == hb_(30-k) by linear-phase symmetry)
hb_1  = -4.962987862434643e-05;   // == hb_29
hb_3  =  0.0006422540255711147;   // == hb_27
hb_5  = -0.002734441417494108;    // == hb_25
hb_7  =  0.00802032401027231;     // == hb_23
hb_9  = -0.019227637930233975;    // == hb_21
hb_11 =  0.0415367046593286;      // == hb_19
hb_13 = -0.09122480814029417;     // == hb_17

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Input gain: discrete LINE / INSTRUMENT / HIGH_GAIN
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `input_gain` is integer 0/1/2; cascade two `==` comparisons.
// 10^(0/20)=1.0   10^(6/20)=1.9952623149688795   10^(12/20)=3.9810717055349722

is_inst = (input_gain == 1);
is_high = (input_gain == 2);
g_in = 1.0
     + is_inst * (1.9952623149688795 - 1.0)
     + is_high * (3.9810717055349722 - 1.0);
g_out = 1.0 / g_in;   // exact small-signal cancellation per design doc §5

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Drive mapping: drive(s) = 1 + 11*s^2  (quadratic taper, design doc §3)
// bias(d)  = 0.05 * max(0, (d-4)/8)
// beta(d)  = 0.20 * max(0, (d-2)/10)
// makeup_gain = 10^(-0.70 * 20*log10(d/tanh(d)) / 20) = (d/tanh(d))^(-0.70)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

s_clamped = clamp(saturate, 0.0, 1.0);
drive = 1.0 + 11.0 * s_clamped * s_clamped;
bias  = 0.05 * max(0.0, (drive - 4.0) * 0.125);   // /8
beta_ = 0.20 * max(0.0, (drive - 2.0) * 0.1);     // /10

// Output makeup: pow(drive/tanh(drive), -0.70). guard against drive==0.
inv_tanh_d = 1.0 / tanh(drive);
ssg        = drive * inv_tanh_d;                  // small-signal gain
makeup     = pow(ssg, -0.70);

// Bypass selector for saturate==0
is_bypass = (saturate <= 0.0);


// =============================================================================
// Channel macro -- replicated for L (suffix _L) and R (suffix _R).
// gen~ codebox does not have user-defined functions in v8, so the chain
// is unrolled per channel.  All History/Delay state is per-channel.
// =============================================================================


// -------------------------- L CHANNEL ----------------------------------------

// Stage 1: input gain
xL = in1 * g_in;

// Stage 2: pre-emphasis biquad (Direct Form I)
History pre_x1_L(0.0);
History pre_x2_L(0.0);
History pre_y1_L(0.0);
History pre_y2_L(0.0);

pre_yL = pre_b0*xL + pre_b1*pre_x1_L + pre_b2*pre_x2_L
       - pre_a1*pre_y1_L - pre_a2*pre_y2_L;
// Denormal flush (cheap on ARM; matters under bypass in x86 deploys)
pre_yL = (abs(pre_yL) < 1e-30) ? 0.0 : pre_yL;

pre_x2_L = pre_x1_L;
pre_x1_L = xL;
pre_y2_L = pre_y1_L;
pre_y1_L = pre_yL;

// Stage 3: 2x upsample (polyphase halfband)
// Delay line of length 16 over the *native-rate* pre-emphasis output.
// pin x_L[n], x_L[n-1], ..., x_L[n-15].
// The "even" up-sample phase is just h[15]*x[n-7]   (with the *2.0 that
// the numpy ref applies to the whole upsampler output to compensate for
// zero-insertion energy loss).  The "odd" phase is the sum of all odd-k
// taps applied in symmetric pairs -- since the FIR is linear-phase
// (h[k]==h[30-k]) we fold pairs together to halve the multiplies.
//
// For the convolution mode='same' alignment used by the numpy ref:
//   y_even[2n]   = h[15] * x[n - 7]
//   y_odd[2n+1]  = sum over odd k:  h[k] * x[n - floor((k-1)/2)]
// We then double both samples (to match the ref's *2.0 normalization).

History uxL_0(0.0); History uxL_1(0.0); History uxL_2(0.0); History uxL_3(0.0);
History uxL_4(0.0); History uxL_5(0.0); History uxL_6(0.0); History uxL_7(0.0);
History uxL_8(0.0); History uxL_9(0.0); History uxL_10(0.0); History uxL_11(0.0);
History uxL_12(0.0); History uxL_13(0.0); History uxL_14(0.0);

// Even-phase output  (sample @ time 2n)
//   delay 7 from current input  -> x[n-7]  -> uxL_6 (since after the shift below
//   uxL_6 holds what was uxL_5 last sample, which was x[n-7] at that point...
// To keep this readable: shift FIRST, then read indices.
//
// State definition AFTER the shift block:
//   uxL_0 = x[n], uxL_1 = x[n-1], ..., uxL_14 = x[n-14], pre_x1_L holds x[n-15]?
// We need x[n-15] for the lower bound of the odd-phase sum. Easiest to use
// 16 delays. But we already have 15 here. Add one more:

History uxL_15(0.0);

// Even phase: tap h[15] * x[n-7]  with *2.0 normalization
yEven_L = 2.0 * hb_15 * uxL_7;

// Odd phase: pair-fold the symmetric odd taps.
//   y_odd = sum over odd k of h[k] * x[n - ((k-1)/2)]
// Pairs (k, 30-k): same coefficient hb_k. The two members of each pair
// read x at delays (k-1)/2 and (30-k-1)/2 = (29-k)/2.
// Concretely:
//   k=1 paired with k=29 -> delays 0 and 14    -> hb_1 * (uxL_0 + uxL_14)
//   k=3 paired with k=27 -> delays 1 and 13    -> hb_3 * (uxL_1 + uxL_13)
//   k=5 paired with k=25 -> delays 2 and 12    -> hb_5 * (uxL_2 + uxL_12)
//   k=7 paired with k=23 -> delays 3 and 11    -> hb_7 * (uxL_3 + uxL_11)
//   k=9 paired with k=21 -> delays 4 and 10    -> hb_9 * (uxL_4 + uxL_10)
//   k=11 paired with k=19 -> delays 5 and 9    -> hb_11 * (uxL_5 + uxL_9)
//   k=13 paired with k=17 -> delays 6 and 8    -> hb_13 * (uxL_6 + uxL_8)
//   k=15 (center) is on EVEN phase, not odd.

yOdd_L = hb_1  * (uxL_0  + uxL_14)
       + hb_3  * (uxL_1  + uxL_13)
       + hb_5  * (uxL_2  + uxL_12)
       + hb_7  * (uxL_3  + uxL_11)
       + hb_9  * (uxL_4  + uxL_10)
       + hb_11 * (uxL_5  + uxL_9)
       + hb_13 * (uxL_6  + uxL_8);
yOdd_L = 2.0 * yOdd_L;

// Stage 4: asymmetric tanh waveshaper applied to BOTH oversampled samples.
// u = sample + bias;  base = tanh(d*u)/tanh(d);  mult = (u<0) ? 1+beta*u : 1
// Out = base * mult.
//
// Even-phase shaped sample:
uE_L     = yEven_L + bias;
baseE_L  = tanh(drive * uE_L) * inv_tanh_d;
multE_L  = (uE_L < 0.0) ? (1.0 + beta_ * uE_L) : 1.0;
shapedE_L = baseE_L * multE_L;
// Odd-phase shaped sample:
uO_L     = yOdd_L + bias;
baseO_L  = tanh(drive * uO_L) * inv_tanh_d;
multO_L  = (uO_L < 0.0) ? (1.0 + beta_ * uO_L) : 1.0;
shapedO_L = baseO_L * multO_L;

// Shift the upsampler input delay line AFTER reading.
// (Ordering: read taps, then shift -- standard FIR pattern.)
uxL_15 = uxL_14;
uxL_14 = uxL_13;
uxL_13 = uxL_12;
uxL_12 = uxL_11;
uxL_11 = uxL_10;
uxL_10 = uxL_9;
uxL_9  = uxL_8;
uxL_8  = uxL_7;
uxL_7  = uxL_6;
uxL_6  = uxL_5;
uxL_5  = uxL_4;
uxL_4  = uxL_3;
uxL_3  = uxL_2;
uxL_2  = uxL_1;
uxL_1  = uxL_0;
uxL_0  = pre_yL;

// Stage 5: 2x downsample (polyphase halfband decimator)
// We push two samples per native sample into the down-sampler delay line.
// The halfband decim FIR has the same taps as the interp FIR.
// Maintain v_L[0..30] = oversampled-shaped stream of length 31.
// Push order each sample: even sample (older time index), then odd sample
// (newer). After both pushes, output one native sample = sum h[k]*v[k].

History dxL_0(0.0);  History dxL_1(0.0);  History dxL_2(0.0);  History dxL_3(0.0);
History dxL_4(0.0);  History dxL_5(0.0);  History dxL_6(0.0);  History dxL_7(0.0);
History dxL_8(0.0);  History dxL_9(0.0);  History dxL_10(0.0); History dxL_11(0.0);
History dxL_12(0.0); History dxL_13(0.0); History dxL_14(0.0); History dxL_15(0.0);
History dxL_16(0.0); History dxL_17(0.0); History dxL_18(0.0); History dxL_19(0.0);
History dxL_20(0.0); History dxL_21(0.0); History dxL_22(0.0); History dxL_23(0.0);
History dxL_24(0.0); History dxL_25(0.0); History dxL_26(0.0); History dxL_27(0.0);
History dxL_28(0.0); History dxL_29(0.0); History dxL_30(0.0);

// Push the two oversampled-shaped samples (FIFO, oldest off the back).
// Two-sample shift in one go. Because the decimator outputs every other
// sample of `convolve(v, h, 'same')[::2]`, pairing the push order
// (shapedE_L then shapedO_L) with reading the symmetric pair sums below
// reproduces the numpy ref exactly.
dxL_30 = dxL_28; dxL_29 = dxL_27;
dxL_28 = dxL_26; dxL_27 = dxL_25;
dxL_26 = dxL_24; dxL_25 = dxL_23;
dxL_24 = dxL_22; dxL_23 = dxL_21;
dxL_22 = dxL_20; dxL_21 = dxL_19;
dxL_20 = dxL_18; dxL_19 = dxL_17;
dxL_18 = dxL_16; dxL_17 = dxL_15;
dxL_16 = dxL_14; dxL_15 = dxL_13;
dxL_14 = dxL_12; dxL_13 = dxL_11;
dxL_12 = dxL_10; dxL_11 = dxL_9;
dxL_10 = dxL_8;  dxL_9  = dxL_7;
dxL_8  = dxL_6;  dxL_7  = dxL_5;
dxL_6  = dxL_4;  dxL_5  = dxL_3;
dxL_4  = dxL_2;  dxL_3  = dxL_1;
dxL_2  = dxL_0;
dxL_1 = shapedE_L;
dxL_0 = shapedO_L;
// (After the shifts, dxL_0 = newest [odd phase], dxL_1 = even phase one
//  oversample-step earlier, ..., dxL_30 = oldest.)

// Decimator output: sum h[k]*v[k] for k=0..30.  Use linear-phase pairing
// to halve the multiplies (h[k] == h[30-k]).
//   k=15 (center): hb_15 * dxL_15
//   even-k pairs (0,30), (2,28), (4,26), ..., (14,16): all zero -- skipped
//   odd-k pairs:  (1,29), (3,27), ..., (13,17)
yDecim_L =
      hb_15 * dxL_15
    + hb_1  * (dxL_1  + dxL_29)
    + hb_3  * (dxL_3  + dxL_27)
    + hb_5  * (dxL_5  + dxL_25)
    + hb_7  * (dxL_7  + dxL_23)
    + hb_9  * (dxL_9  + dxL_21)
    + hb_11 * (dxL_11 + dxL_19)
    + hb_13 * (dxL_13 + dxL_17);

// Stage 6: de-emphasis biquad (Direct Form I)
History de_x1_L(0.0); History de_x2_L(0.0);
History de_y1_L(0.0); History de_y2_L(0.0);

de_yL = de_b0*yDecim_L + de_b1*de_x1_L + de_b2*de_x2_L
      - de_a1*de_y1_L - de_a2*de_y2_L;
de_yL = (abs(de_yL) < 1e-30) ? 0.0 : de_yL;

de_x2_L = de_x1_L;
de_x1_L = yDecim_L;
de_y2_L = de_y1_L;
de_y1_L = de_yL;

// Stage 7: DC blocker  y[n] = x[n] - x[n-1] + R*y[n-1]
History dc_x1_L(0.0);
History dc_y1_L(0.0);

dc_yL = de_yL - dc_x1_L + dcr_R * dc_y1_L;
dc_x1_L = de_yL;
dc_y1_L = dc_yL;

// Stage 8: drive-dependent makeup gain
y_wet_L = dc_yL * makeup;

// Stage 9: INPUT_GAIN output cancellation (linear)
y_wet_L = y_wet_L * g_out;

// Stage X: bypass mux for saturate == 0.
//   Bypass branch: input * g_out  -- input passes through and the
//   INPUT_GAIN cancellation still applies, so output is mode-independent
//   (matches the numpy ref's saturate==0 branch which still applies g_out).
y_dry_L = in1 * g_out;
out1 = is_bypass * y_dry_L + (1.0 - is_bypass) * y_wet_L;


// -------------------------- R CHANNEL ----------------------------------------
// Identical structure to L; independent state.

xR = in2 * g_in;

History pre_x1_R(0.0);
History pre_x2_R(0.0);
History pre_y1_R(0.0);
History pre_y2_R(0.0);

pre_yR = pre_b0*xR + pre_b1*pre_x1_R + pre_b2*pre_x2_R
       - pre_a1*pre_y1_R - pre_a2*pre_y2_R;
pre_yR = (abs(pre_yR) < 1e-30) ? 0.0 : pre_yR;

pre_x2_R = pre_x1_R;
pre_x1_R = xR;
pre_y2_R = pre_y1_R;
pre_y1_R = pre_yR;

History uxR_0(0.0); History uxR_1(0.0); History uxR_2(0.0); History uxR_3(0.0);
History uxR_4(0.0); History uxR_5(0.0); History uxR_6(0.0); History uxR_7(0.0);
History uxR_8(0.0); History uxR_9(0.0); History uxR_10(0.0); History uxR_11(0.0);
History uxR_12(0.0); History uxR_13(0.0); History uxR_14(0.0); History uxR_15(0.0);

yEven_R = 2.0 * hb_15 * uxR_7;

yOdd_R = hb_1  * (uxR_0  + uxR_14)
       + hb_3  * (uxR_1  + uxR_13)
       + hb_5  * (uxR_2  + uxR_12)
       + hb_7  * (uxR_3  + uxR_11)
       + hb_9  * (uxR_4  + uxR_10)
       + hb_11 * (uxR_5  + uxR_9)
       + hb_13 * (uxR_6  + uxR_8);
yOdd_R = 2.0 * yOdd_R;

uE_R     = yEven_R + bias;
baseE_R  = tanh(drive * uE_R) * inv_tanh_d;
multE_R  = (uE_R < 0.0) ? (1.0 + beta_ * uE_R) : 1.0;
shapedE_R = baseE_R * multE_R;

uO_R     = yOdd_R + bias;
baseO_R  = tanh(drive * uO_R) * inv_tanh_d;
multO_R  = (uO_R < 0.0) ? (1.0 + beta_ * uO_R) : 1.0;
shapedO_R = baseO_R * multO_R;

uxR_15 = uxR_14;
uxR_14 = uxR_13;
uxR_13 = uxR_12;
uxR_12 = uxR_11;
uxR_11 = uxR_10;
uxR_10 = uxR_9;
uxR_9  = uxR_8;
uxR_8  = uxR_7;
uxR_7  = uxR_6;
uxR_6  = uxR_5;
uxR_5  = uxR_4;
uxR_4  = uxR_3;
uxR_3  = uxR_2;
uxR_2  = uxR_1;
uxR_1  = uxR_0;
uxR_0  = pre_yR;

History dxR_0(0.0);  History dxR_1(0.0);  History dxR_2(0.0);  History dxR_3(0.0);
History dxR_4(0.0);  History dxR_5(0.0);  History dxR_6(0.0);  History dxR_7(0.0);
History dxR_8(0.0);  History dxR_9(0.0);  History dxR_10(0.0); History dxR_11(0.0);
History dxR_12(0.0); History dxR_13(0.0); History dxR_14(0.0); History dxR_15(0.0);
History dxR_16(0.0); History dxR_17(0.0); History dxR_18(0.0); History dxR_19(0.0);
History dxR_20(0.0); History dxR_21(0.0); History dxR_22(0.0); History dxR_23(0.0);
History dxR_24(0.0); History dxR_25(0.0); History dxR_26(0.0); History dxR_27(0.0);
History dxR_28(0.0); History dxR_29(0.0); History dxR_30(0.0);

dxR_30 = dxR_28; dxR_29 = dxR_27;
dxR_28 = dxR_26; dxR_27 = dxR_25;
dxR_26 = dxR_24; dxR_25 = dxR_23;
dxR_24 = dxR_22; dxR_23 = dxR_21;
dxR_22 = dxR_20; dxR_21 = dxR_19;
dxR_20 = dxR_18; dxR_19 = dxR_17;
dxR_18 = dxR_16; dxR_17 = dxR_15;
dxR_16 = dxR_14; dxR_15 = dxR_13;
dxR_14 = dxR_12; dxR_13 = dxR_11;
dxR_12 = dxR_10; dxR_11 = dxR_9;
dxR_10 = dxR_8;  dxR_9  = dxR_7;
dxR_8  = dxR_6;  dxR_7  = dxR_5;
dxR_6  = dxR_4;  dxR_5  = dxR_3;
dxR_4  = dxR_2;  dxR_3  = dxR_1;
dxR_2  = dxR_0;
dxR_1 = shapedE_R;
dxR_0 = shapedO_R;

yDecim_R =
      hb_15 * dxR_15
    + hb_1  * (dxR_1  + dxR_29)
    + hb_3  * (dxR_3  + dxR_27)
    + hb_5  * (dxR_5  + dxR_25)
    + hb_7  * (dxR_7  + dxR_23)
    + hb_9  * (dxR_9  + dxR_21)
    + hb_11 * (dxR_11 + dxR_19)
    + hb_13 * (dxR_13 + dxR_17);

History de_x1_R(0.0); History de_x2_R(0.0);
History de_y1_R(0.0); History de_y2_R(0.0);

de_yR = de_b0*yDecim_R + de_b1*de_x1_R + de_b2*de_x2_R
      - de_a1*de_y1_R - de_a2*de_y2_R;
de_yR = (abs(de_yR) < 1e-30) ? 0.0 : de_yR;

de_x2_R = de_x1_R;
de_x1_R = yDecim_R;
de_y2_R = de_y1_R;
de_y1_R = de_yR;

History dc_x1_R(0.0);
History dc_y1_R(0.0);

dc_yR = de_yR - dc_x1_R + dcr_R * dc_y1_R;
dc_x1_R = de_yR;
dc_y1_R = dc_yR;

y_wet_R = dc_yR * makeup * g_out;
y_dry_R = in2 * g_out;
out2 = is_bypass * y_dry_R + (1.0 - is_bypass) * y_wet_R;

// =============================================================================
// END tl_saturate.gendsp
// =============================================================================
